#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
COS - Channel Operating System  "Druman"
TTY-native desktop shell.  Part of TCF (The Coloradan Financial).

New in v2.4
-----------
  * Mouse support. Every pointing device is read at once, so a plugged-in
    USB mouse and the built-in touchpad both drive the cursor with nothing
    to configure. Wheel scrolling, real left/middle/right buttons, and a
    separate speed setting, because a mouse sends relative counts and has
    no pad geometry to calibrate against. The duplicate "Mouse" node that
    most laptop touchpads also publish is detected and ignored.
  * Ctrl, Alt and Super are COS's to use in a bare TTY: ^F ^E ^A ^C ^S ^W
    ^P open the menus, Super+Q quick-quits a window, Ctrl+Alt+Q quits COS.

Earlier
-------
  * Direct trackpad support via raw Linux evdev (/dev/input/eventN).
    Works in a bare TTY with no X and no gpm. Tap-to-click,
    two-finger scroll, two-finger right-click, clickpad press.
  * Real window manager: overlapping windows, z-order, focus, taskbar,
    desktop icons, maximize, and Windows-2000-style OUTLINE DRAG
    (drag shows a wireframe; the window teleports on release).
  * Sound engine. Synthesizes its own WAV set on first run and plays
    notification blips through pw-play / aplay / ffplay.
  * Druman -- the COS music AND VIDEO player (drum + man = druman).
    Video is decoded by ffmpeg and rendered into character cells, so it
    plays inside its window on a bare console with no framebuffer access.
  * Image viewer, built on the same cell renderer. Zoom, pan, rotate.
  * Either renderer, per preference: AsciiPic/AsciiVid in the window, or
    hand the screen to ffplay. COS puts the console into KD_GRAPHICS for
    the latter, which fixes the classic blank-until-you-switch-VT bug.
  * Quality settings including inter-cell dithering, a frame-rate cap, and
    pre-rendering a whole video before playback. Cell aspect ratio is
    measured from the panel size, and console fonts can be switched to
    give the renderer more cells.
  * Webinator -- a text-mode web browser with its own HTML renderer.
  * A boot splash that reports what start-up is waiting on, and an
    optional login screen (a screen lock for the look of it, not security).
  * A .cosapp plugin system: drop a Python file in ~/.cos/apps and it
    becomes a COS app, with hot reload and an App Manager. Apps get the
    window toolkit plus notice/warning/error dialogs and a loading bar
    that can drive a worker thread.
  * Pigeon -- shares a folder read-only over the LAN with a hash manifest,
    and serves its own sync client, so the other machine pulls only what
    actually changed instead of copying everything to a USB stick.
  * A customisable desktop: 14 wallpaper patterns, independent colours,
    ANSI-art banners (it will happily use your own cos.txt), icon
    layouts, and twelve pointer shapes including console-style invert.
  * Better tools: real file manager (copy/move/delete/rename/exec),
    text editor, scientific calculator, live system monitor,
    process manager with kill, disk/network panels.

Usage
-----
  python3 'cos(10).py'                  start COS
  python3 'cos(10).py' --grant-input   add yourself to the 'input' group
  python3 'cos(10).py' --test-input    raw pointer event dump
  python3 'cos(10).py' --test-sound     synthesize + play the sound set
  COS_SILENT=1 python3 'cos(10).py'     start with all sound suppressed
  python3 'cos(10).py' --no-apps        start without loading any .cosapp
  python3 'cos(10).py' --crash-test     show the fatal error screen on purpose
  COS_NO_APPS=1 python3 'cos(10).py'    the same, via the environment
  python3 'cos(10).py' --serve [dir] [port]   file server only, no desktop
  python3 'cos(10).py' --mouse-bridge <dev>   internal: sudo pointer helper
"""

